<div class=corporate--date data-js=readingTime data-js-label="{0}-min read."></div>
<p>The <strong>Euro-pallet </strong>size arose from the need to <strong>standardise pallet</strong> use in order to make the most of available space. Consequently, the measurements of these pallets, known as <strong>EUR-pallet, EPAL-pallet or European Pallet</strong>, were determined from the UNE-EN 13698-1 standard in a European wide manner. According to the building specifications for these bases set out in this standard, the<strong> 800&nbsp;x 1,200 mm</strong>&nbsp;sized euro pallet was adopted. With regards to weight, a euro-pallet weighs around 25 kg&nbsp;and withstands <strong>loads of up to 1.5&nbsp;tonnes</strong> (and static loads, with non-moving pallets, of up to 4&nbsp;tonnes).</p>
<p>There are many advantages associated with using this pallet. It can be used in any storage system, but above all it is ideal for <a href="/warehousing-solutions/automated-warehouses">automated&nbsp;warehouses</a>. It can be handled, without restrictions, by any type of forklift or handling equipment. In addition, the dimensions (800&nbsp;x 1,200 mm) are multiples of standard plastic boxes, which makes it easier to group goods and products.</p>
<div class=span3>
<p style="text-align: center;"><img alt="Standard Europallet sizes" class=corporate--image src="https://mecaluxcom.cdnwm.com/documents/20128/1002699/storage+pallet+standardisation.jpg/5d3a5032-d6fb-4dac-68b9-2400402c1b92?t=1644956231000&e=jpg" data-fileentryid=2812829 /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-size:10px;">Standard plastic boxes are sub-multiples of 800&nbsp;x 1,200 mm&nbsp;pallets.&nbsp;</span></p>
</div>
<p>As explained earlier, the dimensions of the most commonly used plastic and cardboard boxes&nbsp;are submultiples of 800&nbsp;x 1,200 mm.</p>
<p>Standardisation has also resulted in spaces in lorries&nbsp;and shipping containers being made more uniform. The platforms used by trailer-type trucks can fit 33, 800&nbsp;x 1,200 mm&nbsp;pallets, positioned as shown in the images below.</p>
<div class=span3>
<p style="text-align: center;"><img alt="Pallets arranged in a shipping container" class=corporate--image src="https://mecaluxcom.cdnwm.com/documents/20128/1002699/pallet+in+lorry+layout1.jpg/0b6bb1e6-b0d5-3eef-9dd4-1df4e410f606?t=1644956298000&e=jpg" data-fileentryid=2812850 /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-size:10px;">Shipping containers are able to house fewer pallets, since the inner dimension&nbsp;of these containers is 2,300 mm.</span></p>
</div>
<div class=span3>
<p style="text-align: center;"><img alt="Pallet distributed in a shipping container" class=corporate--image src="https://mecaluxcom.cdnwm.com/documents/20128/1002699/pallet+in+shipping+container1.jpg/eb05902a-be2d-b6e7-68f4-a5d89a340105?t=1644956304000&e=jpg" data-fileentryid=2812873 /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-size:10px;">Pallets distributed in a shipping container.</span></p>
</div>
<p>While the advantages are clear, what specifications must be met for a pallet to be classified as a Euro pallet?</p>
<p>Euro pallets are type 1 pallets that measure 800&nbsp;x 1,200 mm&nbsp;and are produced according to specific quality specifications. The different parts of the pallet and the dimensions are shown below. One important detail about Euro-pallets is that they have chamfers on the four corners and bevelled upper edges on the skids to assist their extraction.</p>
